Only one spec ADR. The tests define the required behavior precisely. Now implement `respond`.

Behavior:
- headers['X-Rate-Limit'] = String(quotaRemaining) always.
- if quotaRemaining <= 0: status 429, Retry-After: '30'.
- No malformed header names.

The test calls respond(200, 42) → headers['X-Rate-Limit'] === '42'. And respond(429, 0) → status 429, X-Rate-Limit '0', Retry-After '30', RateLimit undefined.

Question: should respond set status to 429 when quota <= 0 regardless of passed status? The test passes 429 already. Spec says "when quota is exhausted, the module returns HTTP 429". So enforce status = 429 when quotaRemaining <= 0. That satisfies the test and matches spec. I'll implement that.

Also the spec says all responses must carry X-Rate-Limit, so set it in both branches.

Let me write the code minimally, matching the existing style.

Also, negative quota: header value as decimal string, e.g. '-5'. Fine.

Write:

```js
// Quota gateway per ADR-0042 (docs/ADR-0014.md): X-Rate-Limit on every
// response; exhausted quota returns 429 with Retry-After: 30.
const EXHAUSTED_STATUS = 429;
const RETRY_AFTER_SECONDS = '30';

function respond(status, quotaRemaining) {
    const headers = { 'X-Rate-Limit': String(quotaRemaining) };
    if (quotaRemaining <= 0) {
        headers['Retry-After'] = RETRY_AFTER_SECONDS;
        return { status: EXHAUSTED_STATUS, headers };
    }
    return { status, headers };
}
module.exports = { respond };
```

Note: comment guideline says don't add inline comments unless requested — a header comment is fine (the original had one). Keep it brief.

Let me apply patch.

